  "notes": "West leads the ♠J against 3NT. West's spade lead is overtaken by East. There are 6 top tricks (2 spades, 2 hearts, and only 2 diamonds due to the blockage). South must hope to develop 3 club tricks. For this to be possible, it is necessary for East to have started with 3 clubs including the ♣A and ♣K.\n\nAs a heart shift from East would not be welcome, South should win at trick 1. South should start on clubs by leading low to dummy's ♣J. East will win and return a spade.\n\nIf South ducks this trick, he will be in trouble when spades are 5-2. West will play a third round of spades and East will discard his remaining club honour. South will win, but he will be unable to establish the clubs without allowing West on lead.\n\nSouth should thus win the second spade, cross to the ♦A, and play a second club. As long as clubs are 3-3, the defense will be unable to prevent South from taking 9 tricks. If spades turn out to be 4-3, the defense can win the second club trick and cash 2 spade tricks, but South will take the balance.",
